  "preamble": "Old Persian turned the Proto-Iranian voiced alveolar affricate *j [dz] (from Proto-Indo-Iranian *ȷ́ and *ȷ́ʰ) into the plain stop d, losing the fricative release. This is one of the features that set Old Persian and its descendants apart from Avestan and most other Iranian languages, which have z here, just as Old Persian has θ where they have s. Middle and New Persian keep the d (Persian dast 'hand', daryā 'sea').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deaffrication, Stopping",
